-		emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
+	if (rn != ARM_R1)
+		emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
+	if (rm != ARM_R0)
+		emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```

Code statements:
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `emit_udiv()` is called with `rn` set to `ARM_R0` (r_scratch). When `rm` is loaded into `ARM_R0` first, it results in both the dividend and divisor being the same, leading to incorrect division results for the BPF instructions `BPF_ALU | BPF_DIV | BPF_K`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (rn != ARM_R1)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R1, rn), ctx);
if (rm != ARM_R0)
    emit(ARM_MOV_R(ARM_R0, rm), ctx);
```
Reason:
The fix involves loading `rn` into `ARM_R1` before loading `rm` into `ARM_R0`. This prevents the overlap of registers, ensuring that `ARM_R0` (used as the divisor) does not get overwritten by `rm`, thus avoiding the situation where both the dividend and divisor are the same, leading to correct division results.

